WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.210
Animators use a variety
of tools for accurately

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.830
enveloping characters.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.050
However, the models
produced by these tools

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.440
are often too slow for use in
high-performance applications,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.710
such as training
simulations and video games.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.570
We propose a technique
that can learn

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.210
from a set of
exported example poses

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.340
and generate a fast
and accurate model

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.410
for high-speed evaluation.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.600
Our model is also
much more expressive

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.790
than linear blend
skinning, currently

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:35.930
the most common enveloping model
for real-time applications.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.810
We tested our technique
on a variety of examples,

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.870
including muscle bulging,
shoulder deformation,

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.970
skin sliding, and twisting.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.790
We can accurately express
joints with more than 1 degree

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.950
of freedom, like the shoulder.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:53.930
Here, we illustrate rotations
along all 3 degrees of freedom.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:57.320
Whereas shoulder is often
cause problems for SSD,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.570
our result is almost
indistinguishable

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.690
from the ground truth.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.860
Our model can accurately
approximate the deformations

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.730
from human scan data.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.380
Here, we demonstrate
our technique,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.270
blending between
human scan poses.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.170
Our model can express large
muscle bulges as well,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:21.730
such as at the knee of this leg,
and roughly approximate skin

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:22.900
sliding over bone.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.380
Here, we have no problems
modeling the large arms

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.420
and shoulders of
the gorilla figure,

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.060
while SSD causes
collapsing artifacts.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.370
Our model even works
well with this set

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.290
of artist-generated
extreme poses.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.110
There has been a rich
body of prior work

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.500
that has tried to address
the deficiencies of SSD.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.389
Displacement
interpolating techniques,

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:11.990
such as post-based defamation,
add corrective displacements

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:15.800
to an existing SSD model, but
generalize poorly for cases

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.850
in which SSD cannot approximate
the rotational deformations.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.610
Our model correctly captures
the twisting present

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.040
in both the bar and the elbow.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.220
Twisting motion is especially
troublesome for SSD,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.160
requiring the displacement
interpolation techniques

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.950
to capture extreme
corrective displacements.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.890
Because our model captures
rotations much better,

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.560
we complement existing
displacement interpolation

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:44.030
techniques by providing
an alternative to SSD.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.760
Previous work has also
inserted halfway joints

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.270
to try to prevent blending
of large rotations.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.540
Because this approach inserts
these joints a priori,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.730
it does not always
generalize well.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:59.840
For instance, consider an
anatomically inspired arm rake

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.630
in which the elbow
both bends and twists.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.190
We compare our
technique with that

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.620
of Mohr and Gleicher and SSD.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.490
The twisting motion is enough
to impair the SSD prediction.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.590
Mohr and Gleicher's
technique does better,

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.540
but there are still differences.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.520
Our result is almost
indistinguishable

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.560
from the ground truth.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.970
Finally, our model generalizes
well to unseen poses.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.070
In this example,
we show our model

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.880
evaluated on a completely
novel test sequence.

